WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:05.325
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.325 --> 00:00:11.580
&gt;&gt; Two years ago on her birthday, a friend
shared something I will ever forget.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.260
For her privacy, I'll call her Ema.

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:21.479
Ema shared, I'm incredibly blessed and
thankful to have made it to 30 years old.

00:00:21.479 --> 00:00:25.415
I don't know much about my kidney donor,
except for

00:00:25.415 --> 00:00:29.896
his cause of death and that he and
I were the same age, 29.

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:33.727
No one prepares you for
the roller coaster of emotions,

00:00:33.727 --> 00:00:38.960
of pure excitement and guilt you feel
when we're saving an organ donation.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:44.780
Today on my birthday,
all I can do is pray for him.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.399
I promise I will never forget
what a gift you have given me.

00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:56.230
Organ donation is a tough topic.

00:00:56.230 --> 00:01:00.545
Not only is it immensely personal,
but we have to talk about death.

00:01:00.545 --> 00:01:05.031
Something very few of us like to
think about let alone discuss, but

00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:08.087
we need to have more of
these conversations.

00:01:08.087 --> 00:01:13.160
The more we can discuss death and
organ donation, the more likely we

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:19.000
are to register as donors and
it's imperative that we have more donors.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.940
Over 115,000 people in America
are on transplant waitlists,

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.310
it is not a fun place to be.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:31.720
Your health is deteriorating while
you're hoping for a donated organ.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:36.500
Every time the phone rings,
there's a rush of adrenaline.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.210
Is there finally an organ for me?

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.520
For thousands, time runs out
before that phone call comes.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:50.390
What's shocking is that the likelihood
of getting that phone call and

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:55.120
receiving an organ transplant
vary significantly by region.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:59.350
Organs are viable outside of the body for
only a short window of time.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:03.060
So the organ donation
network is set up regionally.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.700
When an organ becomes available,

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:09.140
it's offered to the person at the top
of the waitlist in that region.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:13.170
This means people who live in areas
with lots of donors have a much

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:17.379
better chance at a new life than those
who live in areas with few donors.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:24.520
Here in California, we have some of
the best hospitals in the world, but

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:28.755
it is not the best place if
you need an organ transplant.

00:02:28.755 --> 00:02:32.220
Waitlists are some of
the longest in the country.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.050
This is why Steve Jobs got his
liver transplant in Tennessee.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:41.084
At the time, the waitlist for a liver
in Tennessee was under six months while

00:02:41.084 --> 00:02:46.529
the national average was over a year and
some areas had waitlist over three years.

00:02:46.529 --> 00:02:51.311
People are allowed to relocate,
to capitalize on shorter waitlists.

00:02:51.311 --> 00:02:57.190
But very few people have the resources,
time or health to do so.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:03.439
The vast majority are on the waitlist at
the transplant center closest to them.

00:03:03.439 --> 00:03:06.754
In California and across the country,

00:03:06.754 --> 00:03:11.482
we simply don't have enough
organs to meet the demand.

00:03:11.482 --> 00:03:18.112
Less than 54% of us residents here in
California are registered as donors.

00:03:18.112 --> 00:03:23.644
This is below the national average and
well below regions like Montana,

00:03:23.644 --> 00:03:28.460
and Alaska where over 80% of
the population is registered.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.670
Underlying these geographical
differences are differences by race and

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:36.630
recency of emigration.

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:41.510
Minorities and people who have emigrated
recently tend to donate less frequently.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:46.647
So populations like
California that are more

00:03:46.647 --> 00:03:50.746
heterogeneous tend to have
lower donation rates.

00:03:50.746 --> 00:03:52.944
This is particularly concerning.

00:03:52.944 --> 00:03:58.513
Because to get a transplant, you not only
to get to the top of the waitlist, but you

00:03:58.513 --> 00:04:04.438
have to match with the donated organ based
on blood type, tissue biomarkers and size.

00:04:04.438 --> 00:04:08.880
Many of these characteristics
are correlated to race.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:14.020
So matches are much more likely to be
found within our own respective ethnic and

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:14.910
racial groups.

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:21.876
Our geographic and
racial communities need us.

00:04:21.876 --> 00:04:25.440
By being an organ donor,
we have the opportunity to go on and

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.522
save up to eight people's lives.

00:04:27.522 --> 00:04:31.527
These people might be a burn victim
who needs a skin transplant.

00:04:31.527 --> 00:04:36.472
A neighbor with type one diabetes
who needs a kidney or pancreas, or

00:04:36.472 --> 00:04:41.175
a classmate with a severe viral
infection who needs a new heart.

00:04:41.175 --> 00:04:45.350
One act of generosity
can go a very long way.

00:04:48.247 --> 00:04:53.754
As you consider being a donor, I'd
encourage you to have two conversations.

00:04:53.754 --> 00:04:58.569
One with yourself and
one with your loved ones.

00:04:58.569 --> 00:05:02.554
The conversation with yourself,
make sure your questions are answered.

00:05:02.554 --> 00:05:08.067
Being an organ donor is an amazing
opportunity, but it can be on nerving.

00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:13.005
And for many of us, the first time we
consider being an organ donor is at

00:05:13.005 --> 00:05:16.439
the DMV of all places who
feels introspective and

00:05:16.439 --> 00:05:19.893
charitable after waiting in line for
two hours.

00:05:19.893 --> 00:05:25.598
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; When I was first brung the question

00:05:25.598 --> 00:05:30.400
of being an organ donor at the DMV,
I checked the box, no.

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:37.229
At 17, I couldn't shake that doctors
wouldn't work as hard to save me if they

00:05:37.229 --> 00:05:39.750
knew my organs could be used elsewhere.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:44.642
I had yet to get the reassurance that the
priority of the healthcare system is to

00:05:44.642 --> 00:05:47.533
save our lives whether we're donors or
not and

00:05:47.533 --> 00:05:52.299
that the doctors treating us don't even
have access to the donor registry.

00:05:52.299 --> 00:05:57.234
That's all managed by
an external nonprofit.

00:05:57.234 --> 00:06:00.550
My situation was probably not unique.

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:05.282
We need to have these
conversations with ourselves on our

00:06:05.282 --> 00:06:09.432
own time when we can get
the reassurances we need.

00:06:09.432 --> 00:06:16.642
We can then go and register at the DMV or
online at organdonor.gov

00:06:19.312 --> 00:06:24.090
I'm guessing many of you are wondering
why have this personal conversation now.

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:27.040
I'm young and
don't plan to die anytime soon.

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:32.860
I surely hope that's the case for
all of us in the room, but

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:36.690
I'd argue that it's a lot easier
to think about mortality now

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:41.440
when it feels a long way off and
to get comfortable with our decisions.

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:47.575
Being young gave me the opportunity
to reconsider my initial no.

00:06:47.575 --> 00:06:51.826
And as of last year, makes me a proud yes.

00:06:55.427 --> 00:06:59.386
The second conversation is
probably the tougher one.

00:06:59.386 --> 00:07:03.320
The conversation with our
significant others and families.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:10.880
If we're ever in a situation where our
organs can be donated, we'll be dying and

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:14.579
our families will be having one of
the toughest days of their lives.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:20.530
We can make this a little bit easier on
them by clarifying our desires in advance.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:24.390
This is incredibly important.

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:29.627
Hundreds of registered donors don't get
the opportunity to donate their organs,

00:07:29.627 --> 00:07:33.385
because their families insist
otherwise at the hospital.

00:07:33.385 --> 00:07:40.059
We need to prepare them to
make the decisions we want.

00:07:40.059 --> 00:07:44.493
Similarly, let's ask them what
they want for themselves.

00:07:44.493 --> 00:07:49.155
Let's ask them what questions they
have and help them answer them, and

00:07:49.155 --> 00:07:53.358
make sure we are prepared to
facilitate their wishes if needed.

00:07:59.323 --> 00:08:02.716
I find organ donation one
of the most humbling and

00:08:02.716 --> 00:08:06.260
inspiring statements of human kindness.

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:11.124
One person giving the gift
of life to others like Ema.

00:08:11.124 --> 00:08:15.143
I hope that we all have long and
healthy lives, but

00:08:15.143 --> 00:08:18.120
unpredictable situations happen.

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:20.718
Have the conversation with yourself.

00:08:20.718 --> 00:08:24.660
Have the conversation
with your loved ones and

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:29.019
let's step up, and
be there for our communities.

00:08:29.019 --> 00:08:29.959
Thank you.

00:08:29.959 --> 00:08:33.263
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:33.263 --> 00:08:35.962
[MUSIC]

